const CACHE = 'artzfolio-hrms-v518-2026-08-06'; // v518: settings fast-path (boot-only, 2 call sites) + Phase 2.1 RLS hardening (database-side, security advisor now zero findings). No SW/caching-strategy change itself. // v517: renderDashboard honest-failure fix -- a GAS timeout no longer renders a fabricated 0/empty dashboard. HTML-only.; // v516: employees_roster_v boot fast-path (getEmployees) + sbAutoSyncTick trigger detune 5->15min for GAS concurrency contention. HTML/SW no other logic change beyond version.; // v515: PendingAttendance+Reimbursements added to mirror; stale backup-sync trigger re-armed live. HTML/SW no logic change beyond version.; // v514: employees_public->employees_roster_v dead-table fix, HTML-only, no SW logic change; // v513: syncgrid-tooltips-liveaudit — bump only, no cache-logic change; //v512_admin-console-descriptions 2026-08-05. No SW logic change — cache key bumped v511->v512 for 4-way lockstep. //v511_supabase-boot-reads-stage1 2026-08-05. No SW logic change — cache key bumped v510->v511 for 4-way lockstep (see HTML CACHE changelog for the full v511 scope). //v502_exit-type-and-attendance-safety-card 2026-08-05. FIX-1, AND IT WAS A SILENT DATA FAULT THE OWNER FOUND BY ASKING A QUESTION: 'how do I mark somebody terminated, or absconding?' The answer was that you could not. The Direct Exit button sent exitType 'Mutual' HARD-CODED, so every immediate exit - a dismissal for cause, an absconding, a contract simply ending - was filed as a mutual separation on the Exits and Notices record, in the F&F, and in every export made from them. The free-text reason might have been right; the CLASSIFICATION was always wrong, for everybody, and nothing on screen revealed it. Direct Exit now ASKS - Resigned, Terminated, Absconding, Mutual, Contract Ended, Retired - defaults to nothing so it cannot be answered by accident, refuses to run until it is answered, states the choice in the confirmation, and the backend stamps it on the EMPLOYEE record as well as the notice row, so a report can finally tell a termination from a resignation. The Employees sheet already had an exitType column; this path had simply never written to it. The two other exit routes are unchanged and were already correct: 'Initiate Notice / Exit' for a resignation or termination served with notice, and Approvals - Exit Alerts - 'Archive to FnF draft' for an absconding the system has detected from consecutive unapproved-leave days, which already records Absconding. FIX-2, FINISHING v501'S SAFETY NET. The auto-UL circuit breaker - the check that stopped the system marking 26 of 29 staff absent on a day the app was down - lived entirely in the Config sheet: two thresholds nobody could see and a blocked-day alert that could only be cleared by hand-editing a cell. By this project's own rule that is not a finished feature. There is now a card, 'Attendance Safety - stop a bad day marking everyone absent', under Settings - Shifts & Time, showing whether a day is currently blocked and why, with the two thresholds editable and a button to clear the alert once the day has been set by hand. NOTHING about salary, attendance, leave or payslip logic is changed: calcMonthlySalary is md5-identical for the fourteenth consecutive release - BUT SAID PRECISELY, because an audit caught the sentence being broader than the truth: choosing ABSCONDING does change the F&F settlement. That is existing, deliberate policy in handleCalcFnF - absconding forfeits leave encashment and adds the uninformed-absence penalty of roughly three days pay - and it simply never fired on this route before, because the route always said 'Mutual'. The confirmation now spells that out before it is agreed to, and the F&F PREVIEW button sends the same classification as the save, so the figure you check is the figure you get. AUDIT FIXES INSIDE THIS RELEASE, two of them blockers, both the same shape - a card that reports success while doing nothing: getSettings returns an ARRAY of key/value rows and the card read it as an object, so it always showed the built-in defaults and printed a green 'no day has been blocked' even on a day one HAD been blocked; and the three new config keys were never added to the backend's allowed-keys list, so handleUpdateSettings silently rejected every write and still answered ok:true - Save and Clear were decorative. Both fixed, and both buttons now check what actually happened, distinguish a timeout from a failure, and refuse to show anything at all if the reply is not the shape it should be. Also fixed: one vocabulary for exit types, matching the values Initiate Notice already stores, so a report cannot end up treating Resigned and Resignation as two things. // v501_slip-bundle-and-auto-ul-breaker 2026-08-04. FIX-1, WHY 'ALL SLIPS' TOOK EIGHT MINUTES - and it was never the PDF. jsPDF, html2canvas and JSZip all run in this browser and are fast. Building each slip fired THREE separate Apps Script calls, once per employee: the day-by-day projection, the leave balances and the reimbursements. For 29 staff that is about 87 round trips at 7-24 seconds each against a backend that allows four at a time - precisely the eight to fifteen minutes measured live on 4 August, behind a 'Please wait' that said nothing. All three are now fetched for EVERYONE in one chunked call (new backend action getSlipBundleBulk), and each slip is seeded with the answer so the three existing per-employee enrichers short-circuit on their own idempotency guards. No slip layout changes, no figure moves, and if the prefetch fails for any reason the old per-employee path runs exactly as before - slower, never wrong. Applied to all three slip buttons: the combined PDF, the separate PDFs and the ZIP. FIX-2, A REAL PROGRESS BAR. The slip pack now shows 'Preparing salary slips 12 of 29' with a bar and a plain explanation, including while it waits out a busy backend, and the bar is removed on every path including failure. A silent multi-minute wait is indistinguishable from a hang - that is the same lesson this project learned on the payroll screen, applied here. NOTHING about salary, attendance, leave or payslip layout is changed in this file: the only edits are the prefetch helper, the row-seeding helper, the progress helper, three slip call sites and one entry in the silent-actions list. // v500_retry-rounds-and-keep-cancellations 2026-08-04. EVERYTHING IN THIS RELEASE CAME OUT OF RUNNING v499 AGAINST THE LIVE SYSTEM RATHER THAN READING IT. FIX-1, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS: v499 decided that a VOIDED or REJECTED prize row does not count as awarded, so a recompute would create it again. An audit argued for that and the reasoning was plausible. The live sheet says it is wrong. Every row in the production Bonus_Ledger is a Perfect Attendance Bonus for 06/2026 carrying 'VOIDED FULL 2026-07-10 by owner: Poor performance' - the owner deliberately withheld Rs 4,000 from four people, and Perfect Attendance has never excluded voided rows from its own duplicate check, so those four have correctly stayed unpaid. v499 would have made the points prize behave the opposite way: cancel a prize, recompute, and the money comes back. A recompute must NEVER resurrect a payment the owner has cancelled. Voiding is a decision, not a draft. A voided or rejected row now counts as awarded, exactly as Perfect Attendance has always treated it; to genuinely re-award a month, DELETE its prize rows in the Bonus_Ledger sheet - there is no delete-bonus button in the app, only void - or add the corrected prize by hand through Bonus and Loyalty. Both are deliberate acts, which is the point. The concern that motivated v499 - a half-written round frozen forever - is already solved by writing the whole round in ONE atomic call. FIX-2, THE ROUNDS NOW FINISH. Timed against the live backend: v499's 75-second rounds returned nine employees when Google was quiet, four when it was not, and the third round was abandoned by the browser at 150 seconds. No employee is slow - the SAME person failed and then succeeded two minutes apart. Apps Script was queueing, because this account also runs twelve background triggers. At rest between bursts a plain call answers in 5.9 to 8.7 seconds. So: rounds drop to 55 seconds (measured: five employees, 66 seconds of wall clock); there is an eight-second breather between rounds so Google can drain instead of refusing; each round is RETRIED up to three times with a growing pause before the register is declared incomplete; and a payroll round is allowed its own 300-second ceiling instead of the 150 seconds an ordinary button gets. The retry is what actually made the run complete - a round failed twice with a 404 and succeeded on the third attempt, where v499 would have thrown the whole register away. Retrying is safe precisely because of Option B: re-running the same cursor recomputes the same people and a prize already awarded for the month is never written twice. HONEST ESTIMATE, CORRECTED: 15 to 25 minutes for a full month, not the 10 to 15 v499 claimed. PROVEN LIVE THIS ROUND: ten employees accumulated across rounds with ZERO duplicates and strict A-Z order; the cursor, the roster fingerprint and the accumulation all behave; Abhishek Joel computed to 16714.67, matching his July payslip to the paisa. NO salary formula, attendance rule, leave rule, payslip layout, payout value or default is changed: calcMonthlySalary is md5-identical for the release in this chain and in fact unchanged since v464. The one thing that DOES change is a pay-run SETTING, said plainly rather than hidden behind the usual sentence: the default size of a recompute round drops from 75 to 55 seconds and its ceiling from 80 to 70. AUDIT FIXES INSIDE THIS RELEASE, because the first draft of it was withheld too: the retry loop originally retried ANY unsuccessful answer, which would have re-sent a finalised month's polite refusal four times behind the words Google is busy - it now retries ONLY a lost or timed-out reply, never a considered no; a wall-clock deadline of 45 minutes was added, because a round cap alone allowed a theoretical eleven-hour run behind a promise of twenty-five minutes; the abort message now quotes the ceiling that actually applied rather than the general one; and three comments that still described the v499 behaviour this release reverses were deleted. THE 15 TO 25 MINUTES IS AN UPPER BOUND, not a measurement: five employees in 66 seconds implies about eight minutes for 29 people if nothing is dropped, and the wider figure is there to cover the retries that the live run showed are normal. // v499_one-button-payroll-and-option-b 2026-08-04. THE OWNER DECIDED OPTION B: a prize is awarded ONCE, at first approval, and never again. That decision is implemented in the backend, and it is what finally makes a working Recompute All possible. THE ONE BUTTON. Google kills any Apps Script request at SIX MINUTES; a recompute costs about fifteen seconds per employee, so 29 staff need about seven and a half minutes in one request. Recompute All was never slow - IT DID NOT FIT, on every version ever shipped. This screen no longer sends one request: it asks the server to compute as much as fits, take a marker, and carry on from that marker, round after round, until the server says done - showing a progress bar and a plain explanation while it works, because a silent two-minute wait is indistinguishable from a hang. Same engine, same people, same figures; the request is divided, the calculation is not. THREE FAULTS OF THE WITHHELD v497, CLOSED BY CONSTRUCTION: (1) a partial run is never presented as complete - what arrived is labelled INCOMPLETE with the number of people missing and the reason; (2) that warning is rendered INSIDE the register's own banner slot, so the container repaint that erased it in v497 now renders it; (3) Save to Records REFUSES an incomplete register outright, because saveSalaries overwrites the whole month and a part-finished run would leave uncomputed people carrying stale figures. THREE BUTTONS BECOME TWO, and the duplicate is gone: 'Recompute All', 'Recompute Selected' and 'Recompute (live)' were three buttons running TWO commands - 'Recompute (live)' called the identical function as 'Recompute Selected'. There is now one button that does the whole month and one that does only the people you tick, both plainly labelled, and the third has become a whole-month recompute where it sits on the saved register. NOTHING about salary, attendance, leave, timesheets or payslip layout is changed in this file: the only edits are the recompute call, the incomplete banner, the save guard and the button labels. // v498_release-the-stuck-slots 2026-08-04. ONE BUG, AND IT IS WHY THE KIOSK AND THE DASHBOARD STOPPED. This app allows only FOUR backend calls at a time. A call that finishes releases its slot; but the fetch had NO TIMEOUT AT ALL, so a call Google never answers NEVER settles and NEVER releases its slot. Four unanswered calls consume every slot permanently, and from that moment EVERY other call queues behind them forever - the dashboard never finishes loading, the kiosk sits on 'processing', and nothing recovers until the page is closed. That is exactly the reported behaviour: 15-20 minutes for a dashboard and then nothing, and staff unable to clock in. It also explains why a raw call typed into a console answered in 6.9 seconds while the app itself was frozen - the console does not go through the limiter. FIX-1: the WHOLE wait is now budgeted, not just the fetch. The clock starts BEFORE a call queues for a slot, so a call that spent its budget waiting gives up at once instead of starting a fresh wait of its own. Reading the reply body now also happens inside the budget and inside the slot - a reply that stalls half-way through would otherwise hang forever one layer down - and the honest cost of that is that a slot is held a few milliseconds longer than before. The slot is released on EVERY path, including a browser too old to cancel a request at all. AN UNCLAIMED BONUS FOUND BY THE AUDIT: in v496 a call that never settled kept the in-flight counter above zero forever, which PERMANENTLY DISARMED the v493 stuck-curtain watchdog. v496 therefore had the deadlock and a defeated watchdog at the same time. Bounding the call re-arms it. FIX-2, and an audit correctly called this a blocker: a lost answer returns early and was therefore skipping the one toast this app relies on to announce a failure. Twenty-five screens read an answer without checking it first, so a lost answer was landing as a green '0 employees clocked out', a Rs 0 balance, or 'No salary records found'. Every lost answer, every timeout and every command that could not be sent now says so in plain words, once, before it returns. A failure must look like a failure. A SECOND AUDIT CORRECTION INSIDE FIX-2: the first draft reused the eight-action retry list written in v492 for the slow owner buttons, but only ONE of those eight is ever reached on this path - so 27 of the 28 calls a login fires would have had no retry and the app would not have healed without a reload. Any plain read now retries; deliberately NOT anything that merely is not a write, because calcAllSalaries would pass that test and it appends prize rows. AND THE TWO FAILURES ARE NOW TOLD APART, which matters on the factory floor: a request that TIMED OUT may have run, so a punch must not be blindly re-scanned; a request that gave up while queueing NEVER REACHED GOOGLE AT ALL, and the screen now says press it again. FIX-3: the payroll screen catches a thrown call and shows the error instead of spinning forever. FIX-4, stated precisely because a false safety claim has preceded three of the last five incidents: the cache wrapper was swallowing an error thrown by the call itself and then quietly sending the same command again, so a write could be attempted SIX times. It is now at most THREE, and a write that fails that way announces itself instead of clearing its spinner in silence. Repetition on a plain network error is REDUCED, not eliminated. ONE CEILING, ONE PLACE: the slow owner buttons waited 150 seconds while ordinary calls waited forever; both now read a single constant, set to 150s. It is deliberately NOT lower - a 5-employee payroll recompute was measured at 119.7s, so a shorter ceiling would have started killing work that legitimately runs today. WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO, said plainly: it does not make Apps Script fast. If the backend is merely SLOW rather than stuck, a 150s ceiling now turns some very long calls into visible failures that v496 would have waited out - that is the intended trade, because a screen that says so and retries beats a screen that hangs. And Recompute All for all 29 staff still cannot finish inside Google's 6-minute execution limit; that needs the resumable server-side run, which is the next release. NOT IN HERE: the batched Recompute All built as v497 was AUDITED AND WITHHELD - five faults in its own new code, including that batching changes the money because the bonus ledger is snapshotted per request. It is parked in _WITHHELD_v497_do_not_deploy and must not be deployed. No salary formula, attendance rule, leave rule, payslip layout, payout value or default is changed anywhere in this release: calcMonthlySalary is md5-identical and the backend has ZERO logic changes. // v496_month-closes-only-when-everyone-is-paid 2026-08-04. TWO FAULTS, both root-caused from the owner's own screenshot showing a green LIVE FIGURES banner above 'Rs 0 Total Net Payroll, 0 Employees'. FAULT-1, THE EMPTY REGISTER. Apps Script now takes 54-60 seconds on a whole-sheet read and past that point Google ABANDONS the request and answers with its own status page - a body that looks like {status:ok, app:ArtzFolio HRMS v495, version, ts}. That is VALID JSON, so the parse guard added in v492 let it straight through, and the payroll screen's guard tested res.ok === false, which is FALSE for a reply that has no ok field at all. The screen then fell through to res.salaries || [] and rendered an EMPTY register under a green LIVE banner. A failed fetch was presented as a successful payroll run of zero employees - the exact rule this system is built on breaking. FIX: API._rawCall now RECOGNISES the status-page shape and turns it into a real failure (and releases the loading curtain, which the v493 lesson requires), and the payroll screen now demands res.ok === true before it will render a single figure. Anything else is an error message, never an empty register. FAULT-2, THE MONTH THAT CLOSED ITSELF. The owner is right and the old rule was wrong. Since v172 the freeze read: if ANY ONE approved salary row exists for a past month, the WHOLE month is FINALIZED. One employee left, his F&F was generated and saved, and that single row closed July for all 29 people. Worse, an F&F-settled employee is DELIBERATELY EXCLUDED from the monthly register by the v61 double-count guard - so the month was being closed by a row belonging to somebody who is not part of that register at all. NEW RULE, which is the owner's own words: a month is finalised ONLY when every employee who belongs in that register already has an approved salary record. The expected population is the full Active / Notice / Notice Period list MINUS anyone settled through F&F for that month, and it is measured before any employee-picker narrowing so recomputing three people cannot make a month look closed. If even one person is still unpaid the month stays OPEN and recompute proceeds normally, as many times as needed. The freeze itself is kept for the genuine case - a fully paid month is still protected and still overridable. NO salary formula, no attendance rule, no payout value and no default is changed: calcMonthlySalary remains md5-identical. // v495_close-the-salary-leak-for-real 2026-08-03. Carries v493 (frozen-screen fix + watchdog) and v494 (boot-weight removal). THE HEADLINE IS A LIVE P0 THAT I HAD PREVIOUSLY REPORTED AS CLOSED AND HAD NOT. On 2 August the salary leak was 'fixed' by removing the view employees_public from the hrms-read allow-list. That allow-list is a rule inside one Edge Function. It is NOT what PostgREST consults. The view itself still existed, was SECURITY DEFINER, and the anon role still held SELECT on it - and the anon key is printed in the app's own public HTML. So for the last day and a half, anybody on the internet could read every salary and every mobile number with a key that ships in the page. REPRODUCED, not theorised: a plain GET to rest/v1/employees_public with that key returned id, name, salary 14000, mobile 9573943885. CLOSED, and proven closed: employees_public is DROPPED; employees_roster_v and attendance_month_v are now security_invoker=on with SELECT revoked from anon and authenticated; anon now gets permission denied on both and 'table not found' on the dropped one, while the app's own path through hrms-read (service key) still returns 53, 1666, 244 and 1699 rows. THE LESSON, recorded so it cannot happen a third time: an allow-list inside one function is not an access control - it only governs the door it sits on. Postgres grants and RLS govern the database. A leak is not closed until the ORIGINAL attack is re-run and fails. Also this round: the sanitised roster view employees_roster_v was created and PROVEN clean (explicit column list, zero of 34 banned money and PII columns, checked in the schema AND in the live payload) and is allow-listed in hrms-read v8 - but the client code to consume it was REMOVED before shipping, because an independent audit proved it was dead code with no caller on any screen, and because designation is filled in for only 9 of 53 employees and department for 10, so it would have delivered nothing visible today. The view is in place and locked down for when those fields are filled in. This HTML is therefore byte-identical to v494 apart from the two version markers. NOTHING about salary, attendance, leave, timesheets, payslips or payroll logic is touched anywhere in this release. // v494_take-the-weight-off-the-boot 2026-08-03. THIS RELEASE CARRIES v493's OUTAGE FIX AND THEN TAKES WORK AWAY, because the honest finding is that the speed project has been ADDING load, not removing it. MEASURED LIVE TODAY against the real backend: an action with NO HANDLER AT ALL - the dispatcher simply reports unknown action - took 14.6 SECONDS. getSettings took 14.6s. keepWarmStatus took 14.9s cold and 5.6s warm. That is the floor: every single Apps Script call costs about 6 to 15 seconds no matter what it does, so the ONLY thing that makes a screen faster is MAKING FEWER CALLS. And I had been adding them. v473 added a background getReadToken refresh on every boot; v489 added a background getPunchToken refresh on every boot; v489/v491 then made each of those calls ALSO do a Supabase network round trip AND take an Apps Script script lock, on the boot path, competing with clock-ins. AUDIT CORRECTION, because overstating a fix is how the last three went wrong: the honest saving is ONE Apps Script call on most devices and TWO on the owner's, not two everywhere, and ZERO on a first-ever boot where both keys still have to be fetched. Those calls were background work, not on anybody's visible clock. The real win is narrower and still worth having: it frees one or two of the four concurrency slots during the boot storm, and it removes one or two acquisitions of the app-wide script lock that clock-in queues behind. v494 removes exactly that and nothing else: both refreshes are now time-boxed to once every 12 hours, and the repair path is not weakened but sharpened - a key that is actually REFUSED (401 from the cloud) sets a repair flag that forces an immediate re-fetch on the next call and asks the backend to re-push it. A working key is never re-fetched; a broken one is repaired at once. Also carried from v493: the reference-counted Please-wait curtain that froze the app is fixed at its source and given a watchdog floor. NOTHING about salary, attendance, leave, timesheets, payslips or payroll is touched anywhere in this release - the only files changed are the two token refresh paths, one 401 branch, and the version markers. // v493_release-the-stuck-please-wait 2026-08-03. ONE MISSING LINE TOOK THE APP DOWN, AND IT WAS MINE. After v492 the app sat on Please wait forever, across refreshes, for two hours. PROVEN LIVE, not guessed: the backend was healthy the whole time (getSettings answered with valid JSON in 14.6 seconds, serverVersion v492), the page had rendered completely behind the curtain, and there was not one console error. The overlay is REFERENCE COUNTED - show() increments a counter, hide() decrements it, and it only lifts at zero - and the JSON-parse guard I added to API._rawCall in v492 RETURNED from inside its catch without calling hide(). One unparseable reply therefore left the counter stuck above zero and the curtain down permanently, with the working app and the working backend sitting behind it. Forcing the overlay off in the owner's live browser revealed a fully functional app, which is what identified it. FIX-1: that path now hides the overlay before it returns. FIX-2, and this is the one that matters more: a counter that can only be corrected by perfectly balanced code will fail again the next time anybody writes an early return, so it now has a floor under it. A watchdog checks every ten seconds and, if the curtain has been up for more than 90 seconds with no real activity for the last 20, releases it, records the reason, reports it to crash reporting and tells the owner in plain words. A stuck screen is now a 90-second inconvenience instead of an outage. No salary formula, no attendance rule, no payout value and no default is changed anywhere in this release. // v492_undo-the-cachebuster-and-simplify-payroll 2026-08-03. Cache key bumped v491 -> v492. Removes the cb= cache-buster added in v490, which was proven by an A/B test against the live backend to make Apps Script answer a POST with a 404 web page - that is what broke every slow owner button. Also returns the kiosk Who's In/Out panel to the read that carries names, designation and shift, and makes recomputing a finalised payroll month one plain button again. No service-worker or caching-strategy change. Deploy AS sw.js. // v491_settings-snapshot-and-kiosk-fastread 2026-08-03. Cache key bumped v490 -> v491. Every approved payroll register now snapshots the settings it was produced under; the kiosk reads today's attendance through its own punch key instead of Apps Script; and a Check the Cloud Keys button answers whether a key rotation actually went through. Also corrects v490's published root cause: a status-page reply means the REPLY was lost, not that the command did not run. No service-worker or caching-strategy change. Deploy AS sw.js. // v490_four-live-faults 2026-08-03. Cache key bumped v489 -> v490. Four live faults fixed: the slow-button POST that Google answered with its status page, the mirror sync being killed at the 6-minute ceiling, the kiosk warning about UNKNOWN attendance counts after a single failed try, and a FINALIZED payroll month that could not be recomputed and could not tell you whether recomputing would change anything. No service-worker or caching-strategy change. Deploy AS sw.js. // v489_kiosk-gets-its-own-key 2026-08-03. Cache key bumped v488 -> v489. The root cause of the 2 August leak is closed: the punch path now has its OWN key (getPunchToken), so the cloud read key is issued only to a signed-in device and can finally be rotated from a Settings button. No service-worker or caching-strategy change of any kind. Deploy AS sw.js. // v488_payroll-page-shows-payroll 2026-08-02. THE REPORTED BUG, root-caused and fixed. Opening Payroll showed the DASHBOARD instead - sidebar said Payroll, title said Payroll, content was the dashboard - plus a stray warning toast, and it only came right after a third refresh. ROOT CAUSE: _v303TwoPhase is a fast-paint then authoritative-repaint helper. It paints instantly from the cloud copy, then fires a repaint callback when the slow Apps Script answer finally arrives. The dashboard's callback is renderDashboard(), which writes straight into admin-tab-content - and NOTHING checked whether the user was still on that screen. Apps Script takes 5-33 seconds, which is far longer than it takes to click Payroll, so the late answer landed on top of whatever screen had replaced it. Refreshing 'fixed' it only because it left no stale promise in flight. FIX-1: _v303TwoPhase now remembers which screen asked, and silently skips the repaint if the user has navigated away. One guarded change, all four call sites, and behaviour is byte-identical when the user has NOT moved. FIX-2: the kiosk Who-In-Out refresh ran on a global 5-minute timer and raised its warning toast on whatever screen happened to be open - it now does nothing unless its own panel is actually on screen. FIX-3: the Payroll empty state now names the month it is showing and says how to change it, because at the start of a month it defaults to the new month and reads as if the data had vanished. NO setting, salary rule, payout value or default is changed anywhere in this release. //v487_readtoken-login-and-narrow-reads 2026-08-02. (A) SECURITY, the root cause of today's leak. Proven live: the PUBLIC config.json gave the apiToken, that alone unlocked getReadToken, and that key returned every employee's salary, mobile, date of birth and home address. The exposed view was withdrawn immediately (hrms-read v5), but the door itself was still open and Leaves and Attendance were still reachable through it. getReadToken now requires a REAL SIGNED-IN SESSION - either a valid owner token or a valid HMAC role token, both of which every logged-in device already sends on every call (v91 P1). An anonymous caller holding only the public apiToken is refused. Failure is graceful by construction: _v265Init already falls back to Apps Script and _v474FastAck already returns null, so the worst case is slower, never broken. Kill switch Config READTOKEN_REQUIRE_LOGIN = off restores the old behaviour without a redeploy, and refusals are audit-logged once per 10 minutes so a legitimate caller cannot hide. (B) SPEED, the worst read in the app. _v327LoadTodayMap pulled the ENTIRE 1,671-row attendance table into the browser to build a map of TODAY - on the kiosk, on the punch path. It now asks for one day. _sbDirectRead gained optional date-range narrowing matching hrms-read v5 (dateCol/fromDate/toDate); every existing caller passes no range and is byte-identical. NO setting, no salary rule, no payout value, no default and no variable is changed anywhere in this release. //v486_where-pay-comes-from-and-scan-routes 2026-08-02. Two owner-visibility additions, both in the Speed & Health card so nothing has to be hunted for. (A) WHERE YOUR PAY IS CALCULATED FROM. The owner could not find the payroll-engine source setting -- it lives four cards further down in Advanced - Supabase Migration & Speed Control Center, which is a reasonable place to hide a switch and a terrible place to hide an ANSWER. The Speed & Health card now states it in one plain line every time the card is opened. It says Google Sheets, or Cloud copy in red, or -- if the check itself fails -- that it could not tell. It never guesses the safe answer. (B) WHICH ROAD DID RECENT SCANS TAKE. Until now a deferral left no trace anywhere: the Edge Function handed a reason to the browser and the reason died there, which is exactly why four versions were spent hunting a bug that did not exist. hrms-punch v4 now records every deferral fire-and-forget (not awaited, so it adds zero time to a scan), a new PunchDefers table holds them for 30 days, and a new button reports the last 24 hours grouped by reason in plain English. Also shown: the route the last scan on THIS device took. No attendance, salary, leave or payroll logic is touched. //v485_daily-health-report 2026-08-02. (A) DAILY HEALTH REPORT: a new Settings button, 'Send Me the Health Report Now', runs five live overnight checks and emails the verdict; the backend also sends it by itself at 07:00 every morning. The headline is built FROM the findings, and a check that could not be read is shown as a FAILURE, never as a pass. (B) PUNCH PATH VISIBILITY: the kiosk confirmation panel now states, in words, WHICH route the scan took -- Instant (about a quarter of a second) or the normal Google backend -- and when the instant route declined, WHY. Proven live on 2026-08-02: the reason the punch queue stayed empty through v474-v484 was never a bug; every test scan was past the 60-minute late-approval threshold and the Edge Function correctly sent it for approval instead. The system was right and simply never said so out loud. This makes it say so. No attendance, salary, leave or payroll logic is touched. //v484_instantpunch-reaches-the-kiosk 2026-08-02: Instant Punch was wired into the admin and ESS clock-in only, never into the kiosk scan path -- which is why the queue stayed empty and scanning still felt slow. Now connected there too. //v483_instantpunch-label-and-timesheet-autoretry 2026-08-02: the Instant Punch button now shows its real ON or OFF state on load plus a permanent status line, and the Timesheet retries once by itself before showing the could-not-load banner. //v482_failed-fetch-guard 2026-08-02: new shared _v481Trustworthy helper so no screen renders a meaningful value out of a failed or empty fetch; applied first to the kiosk Who-In-Out panel. //v480_punch-reconciliation (2026-08-02): a read-only nightly check plus a Settings button that PROVES every scan the fast path accepted is actually present in the Attendance sheet, naming anything missing by employee and date. //v479_kiosk-stop-after-punch-and-instantpunch-default-on (2026-08-02): the kiosk no longer auto re-arms the scanner after a punch (v432 FIX-B) -- it stops the QR loop, RELEASES the camera and shows a clear confirmation, so the operator taps CLOCK IN / OUT again for the next person. Instant Punch is now ON by default. //v478_p0-triggerfix-and-safe-repair (2026-08-02, URGENT): v477's everyMinutes(3) is illegal in Apps Script (only 1/5/10/15/30) and the self-healer's delete-before-create left the mirror sync with no trigger. Interval 3->5, self-healer now validates the interval and creates before deleting. //v477_syncgate-3min-and-readallowlist (2026-08-02): the Supabase mirror sync now asks Drive one cheap 'has anything changed?' question instead of reading all 17 tables every tick, so the interval drops 15 min -> 3 min while the idle load falls. Read allow-list widened by 6 company-wide reference tables only. //v476_slowbutton-patience-and-sentry-forceload (2026-08-02): the two slow owner buttons now use a dedicated 150s call with a live seconds counter (Check & Repair Triggers was being abandoned before Google answered and wrongly reported a dead backend -- it was healthy), and Sentry.forceLoad() is called after boot because the loader script defers the real SDK until the first crash. Carries v475/v474/v473. //v475_settings-buttons-and-crash-reporting (2026-08-02): new Settings card 'Speed & Health -- Instant Punch, Triggers, Queue' with four real buttons (no console, no function names) + Sentry crash reporting (async, PII-scrubbed, cannot affect the app if blocked). Carries v474 + v473. //v474_instant-punch-and-selfhealing-triggers (2026-08-02): self-healing triggers (doPost repairs Keep-Warm/Auto-Sync/drain hourly -- no manual reinstall ever again) + instant clock-in/out via the new hrms-punch Edge Function and PunchQueue table (~250ms verdict; the authoritative Apps Script write still runs in the background, unchanged; flag az3_fastPunch defaults OFF). Carries v473's 3 fixes. //v473_fastmode-tokencache-timesheet-loudfail-rosterwait (2026-08-02, HTML-ONLY this round -- this file has zero logic changes, version bumped purely for 4-way lockstep): (1) PERF -- the browser's Supabase read-token init awaited a getReadToken GAS round-trip before reading its own localStorage cache, so Fast Mode was effectively OFF for the first ~2 minutes of every session (GAS measured 40-60s per call live on 2026-08-02); now cache-first with a background refresh. (2) Timesheets all-blank/all-A root-caused to a missing empty-result guard in renderTimesheets, NOT data loss -- both data paths verified correct live (Supabase 1.06s / GAS 41.0s); now shows a loud banner + '?' instead of a false 'A'. (3) Bulk Hours Entry / Range Backfill / Bulk Shift 'No employees loaded' -- the modals now fetch the roster on demand instead of bailing. No salary, attendance, leave or payroll logic touched anywhere.  //v469_samedayUL-qrdebounce-fastmode-schema (2026-08-01): version bump for HTML/GAS/SW lockstep -- same-day auto-UL sweep, kiosk QR camera timeout+debounce fix, Supabase Fast-Mode schema gap flagged (see HTML changelog / decision log for full detail). No SW/caching-strategy change. //v460_bulkslip-layout-parity-fix (2026-07-31): version bump for HTML/GAS/SW lockstep -- bulk salary-slip PDF paths (All Slips combined PDF / Separate PDFs / ZIP) now render through the same locked HTML slip renderer the single-slip download uses, see HTML CACHE changelog; no SW/caching-strategy change itself. // v459_grace-adjusted-lateness-note (2026-07-30): version bump for HTML/GAS/SW lockstep -- OT Claims + Bulk Bonus identity display fixes, see HTML CACHE changelog; no SW/caching-strategy change itself. // v455_identity-consistency-fix (2026-07-30): version bump for HTML/GAS/SW lockstep -- Approvals + Payroll picker identity display fixes, see HTML CACHE changelog; no SW/caching-strategy change itself. // v454_ess-fixattendance-whitelist-fix (2026-07-30): version bump for HTML/GAS/SW lockstep -- GAS-only ESS whitelist fix, see GAS HRMS_VERSION changelog; no SW/caching-strategy change itself. // v453_daybyday-slipdownload-diagnostic-fix (2026-07-30): version bump for HTML/GAS/SW lockstep -- fixed the single-slip download's day-by-day table silently showing nothing with no reason (HTML-only, see HTML CACHE changelog); no SW/caching-strategy change itself. // v452_bulkattendance-late-ot-parity (2026-07-30): version bump for HTML/GAS/SW lockstep -- bulk/backfilled attendance entries now get lateMinutes + otMinutes computed the same way a live kiosk punch does (GAS-only fix, see GAS HRMS_VERSION changelog); no SW/caching-strategy change itself. // v451_devicenaming-daybyday-loudfailure-fix // v447_slip-spacing-pagefit-fix
// v435_advance-eligibility-earned-salary-fix (2026-07-26): version bump for HTML/GAS/SW lockstep -- Advance Salary eligibility ceiling (checkAdvanceEligibility) now nets Late/UL/Sandwich/Undertime the same way the Payroll tab does, instead of a raw days-present x daily-rate figure; no SW/caching-strategy change itself; see GAS HRMS_VERSION changelog for full detail.
